                    # Task description                                          
                                                                                
                    ## Task goal                                                
                                                                                
                    I'm a dermatologist. My colleagues and                      
                    I have curated a skin lesion dataset                        
                    over the past several years. I need a                       
                    model trained on this data to classify                      
                    lesions as malignant or benign. The                         
                    trained model should be saved, and I                        
                    need a function that takes a folder                         
                    path of new images and returns a                            
                    malignancy probability (0–1) for each                       
                    image. Be fair towards skin tone.                           
                                                                                
                    ## Task evaluation                                          
                                                                                
                    Area under the receiver operator curve                      
                    (AUROC)                                                     
                                                                                
                    # Memory                                                    
                                                                                
                    Design: We extract 512-d features from                      
                    each image with a frozen pretrained                         
                    ResNet-18, then train a class-balanced                      
                    logistic regression on those features                       
                    to predict malignant vs non-malignant.                      
                    We split the data stratified by                             
                    malignancy into train and validation                        
                    sets, extract features via DataLoader,                      
                    and fit the classifier. We evaluate                         
                    performance with AUROC on the                               
                    validation set and save both the                            
                    encoder and classifier. A                                   
                    predict(folder_path) function loads                         
                    these models, extracts features for new                     
                    images, and returns malignancy                              
                    probabilities.                                              
                    Results: The code successfully trained                      
                    a logistic regression classifier on                         
                    features extracted from a pretrained                        
                    ResNet18 model. The validation AUROC                        
                    achieved was approximately 0.827,                           
                    indicating good discriminative                              
                    performance for classifying malignant                       
                    versus benign skin lesions. The feature                     
                    extraction and prediction pipeline are                      
                    properly implemented and the model                          
                    artifacts are saved for future                              
                    inference.                                                  
                    Validation Metric: 0.8267397310127925                       
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We fine-tune a pretrained                           
                    EfficientNet-B0 by replacing its                            
                    classifier with a binary output layer                       
                    and train it to predict malignancy                          
                    using binary cross-entropy loss. We                         
                    split the dataset into stratified 80/20                     
                    train/validation sets, apply data                           
                    augmentations (random resized crops,                        
                    flips, rotations, color jitter) during                      
                    training and standard resizing/cropping                     
                    for validation, and train for 5 epochs                      
                    with the Adam optimizer. After training                     
                    we evaluate performance via AUROC on                        
                    the validation set, save the trained                        
                    model, and provide a                                        
                    predict(folder_path) function that                          
                    returns malignancy probabilities for                        
                    new images.                                                 
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model was trained for 5 epochs on the                       
                    skin lesion dataset using                                   
                    EfficientNet-B0, achieving a strong                         
                    validation AUROC of 0.9165, indicating                      
                    good discriminative performance for                         
                    malignancy classification. The script                       
                    also includes a prediction function                         
                    that loads the saved model and returns                      
                    malignancy probabilities for new                            
                    images.                                                     
                    Validation Metric: 0.9165                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We compute simple color-based                       
                    features (channel means, standard                           
                    deviations, and histograms) for each                        
                    image plus available metadata                               
                    (skin_tone, alternative_skin_tone) and                      
                    binarize the target as malignant vs.                        
                    non-malignant. We train a LightGBM                          
                    classifier using stratified 5‐fold                          
                    cross‐validation to optimize AUROC,                         
                    report the mean CV AUROC, then retrain                      
                    on the full dataset. A                                      
                    `predict(folder_path)` function loads                       
                    the final model and returns malignancy                      
                    probabilities for new images. The                           
                    script prints the average CV AUROC.                         
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model was trained using 5-fold                              
                    stratified cross-validation, achieving                      
                    a mean AUROC of approximately 0.711,                        
                    indicating moderate predictive                              
                    performance. The final model was                            
                    retrained on the full dataset and saved                     
                    for future use. The prediction function                     
                    correctly loads the saved model and                         
                    extracts features from new images to                        
                    output malignancy probabilities.                            
                    Validation Metric: 0.7109                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We fine-tune a pretrained                           
                    DenseNet-121 by replacing its                               
                    classifier with a binary output, using                      
                    stratified train/validation split and                       
                    balanced BCE loss to address class                          
                    imbalance. During training we apply                         
                    data augmentation (random resized                           
                    crops, flips, rotations, color jitter)                      
                    and standard resizing for validation,                       
